/-!
# Integrality of X⁶j⁴(j-1728)³ bounds orders of hΔ by gE₄²E₆

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.analyticOrderAt_le_of_isIntegral_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq`
(`60906260-f073-5409-b93a-d97c5c431efd`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `7f31c6b9-95c6-59b2-9bee-da1c3bbfdd63`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a natural number, $k$ an integer, and let $g,h$ be modular forms of weight $k$ for
$\Gamma_0(N)$. Let $X$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying $X\cdot\tilde h=\tilde g$ in
$\mathbb{C}((q))$, where $\tilde g,\tilde h$ denote the $q$-expansions of $g,h$ at $\infty$ taken
with period $1$ and viewed as Laurent series. Write $J=$ [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb ℂ
ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) for the image in $\mathbb{C}((q))$ of the
Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot(\mathrm{jNumQ})$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ under the coefficientwise ring map
induced by $\mathbb{Q}\to\mathbb{C}$, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$. Assume
that $X^6J^4(J-1728)^3$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated
by $J$. Then for every $\tau$ in the upper half plane, the analytic order of vanishing at
$\tau\in\mathbb{C}$ of the function $z\mapsto h(z)\Delta(z)$, composed with
`UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex`, is at most the analytic order of vanishing at $\tau$ of $z\mapsto
g(z)E_4(z)^2E_6(z)$, composed with `UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex` (orders taken in
$\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$).

The statement converts an integrality hypothesis for the quotient $X=\tilde g/\tilde h$ over
$\mathbb{C}[j]$ into a pointwise comparison of vanishing orders on the upper half plane, using
$j=E_4^3/\Delta$ and $j-1728=E_6^2/\Delta$. It is the divisor-theoretic input to
[`ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_of_isIntegral`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_of_isIntegral),
where the resulting divisibility produces a cusp form with prescribed $q$-expansion.

